<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick William IV of Prussia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1861">1861</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick William IV was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1840 to 1861. A romantic conservative who admired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, he was forced by the revolution of 1848 to grant a constitution, but in 1849 refused the imperial crown offered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a>. His plan for a union of German states under Prussia was abandoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olmütz</a> in 1850. After strokes, his brother William ruled as regent from 1858.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia.jpg" alt="Frederick William IV of Prussia" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hermann Biow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Wilhelm_IV_of_Prussia_(1847).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the revolutions of 1848–1849, the two princes ceded their principalities to their kinsman, King <b>Frederick William IV of Prussia</b>, and the territories were transferred in 1850, forming the Prussian Hohenzollern Lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1849 <b>Frederick William IV of Prussia</b> refused the imperial crown offered by the Frankfurt Parliament but tried to unite northern Germany in the Erfurt Union, a plan he abandoned under Austrian and Russian pressure in the Punctation of Olmütz in November 1850.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The revolution of 1848 led <b>Frederick William IV</b> to grant a constitution, revised in 1850, which set up a parliament, the Landtag, whose lower house was elected by a three-class franchise weighted by taxes paid.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 1840s it was well known in Lower Saxony and reportedly a favourite of <b>Frederick William IV of Prussia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/silent-night">Silent Night</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick William IV</b> rebuilt the ancestral castle in neo-Gothic style from 1850 to 1867.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hohenzollern-hechingen">Principality of Hohenzollern-Hechingen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1837" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick William IV of Prussia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5300 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1837">What was here in 1837? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
